- (1) the ideal gas model with constant specific heats (use the specific heats at the average temperature, look up Table A-20);
- (2) the ideal gas model with variable specific heats (look up Table A-22);
- (3) using CoolProp to retrieve 𝑢 and ℎ of ideal gas.

At the end of this course, students will be able to
- identify  various  power  cycle  and  refrigeration  cycle  processes and components;
- analyze the efficiency of power cycles and refrigeration cycles;
- determine the sources of inefficiency in power and refrigeration cycles;
- analyze the air-conditioning processes by psychrometrics;
- calculate the energy released by reacting mixtures (focusing on combustion processes);
- code in Python with the CoolProp database for thermodynamic analysis of energy conversion systems.

- No pressure drop of the refrigerant flow passing the heat exchangers;
- The refrigerant undergoes a reversible, adiabatic compression process when passing the compressor.
